# ==============================================================================
# CROSS-SUITE PLATFORM: AUTOMATION, COMPILATION & EXECUTION MANUAL
# ==============================================================================

This document provides complete instructions for executing, compiling, and managing
the Cross-Suite platform using the automated bootstrapping scripts across Linux, 
macOS, and Windows systems.

==============================================================================
==>> 1. QUICK START GUIDE
==============================================================================

=> LINUX & macOS (Terminal / Bash):
   1. Open your terminal and navigate to the project directory:
      cd Cross-Suite_Wizard

   2. Ensure execution permissions are active:
      chmod +x autorun.sh

   3. Execute the standard bootstrapper:
      ./autorun.sh

=> WINDOWS (Command Prompt / PowerShell / File Explorer):
   1. Standard Launch:
      Double-click `autorun.bat` (or run `autorun.bat` from CMD/PowerShell).

   2. Deep Clean & Hard Rebuild:
      Double-click `autorun-hard.bat` (or run `autorun-hard.bat` from CMD/PowerShell).


==============================================================================
==>> 2. BASH ENGINE: autorun.sh (Linux & macOS)
==============================================================================

The `autorun.sh` script automates environment path resolution, toolchain validation, 
code change detection, incremental compilation, and platform execution.

=> AVAILABLE EXECUTION COMMANDS & FLAGS:

   * Standard Execution (Auto-Detect & Launch):
     Command: ./autorun.sh
     Action : Checks dependencies, scans for source code updates, compiles 
              incrementally if changes are detected, and starts the platform.

   * Force Recompile (Fresh Binary Build):
     Commands: ./autorun.sh -f
               ./autorun.sh --force
               ./autorun.sh -b
               ./autorun.sh --build
     Action  : Deletes existing binaries, cleans standard build cache, and forces
              a clean native compilation.

   * Hard Purge & Cache Reset:
     Commands: ./autorun.sh -H
               ./autorun.sh --hard
     Action  : Completely purges Go module caches, clears build caches, forces 
              dependency re-download (`go mod download`), tidies modules, and 
              rebuilds from scratch. Use this if experiencing corrupted dependencies.

   * Universal Multi-Platform Cross-Compilation:
     Commands: ./autorun.sh -a
               ./autorun.sh --all
               ./autorun.sh all
               ./autorun.sh cross
     Action  : Compiles static, standalone binaries for all supported platforms 
              simultaneously and exports them into the `./dist/` folder:
              - dist/Cross-Suite_Wizard-linux-amd64 (Linux x86_64)
              - dist/Cross-Suite_Wizard-linux-arm64 (Linux ARM64 / Raspberry Pi / AWS)
              - dist/Cross-Suite_Wizard-darwin-amd64 (macOS Intel)
              - dist/Cross-Suite_Wizard-darwin-arm64 (macOS Apple Silicon M1/M2/M3/M4)
              - dist/Cross-Suite_Wizard-windows-amd64.exe (Windows 64-bit with Manifest)


==============================================================================
==>> 3. WINDOWS ENGINES: autorun.bat & autorun-hard.bat
==============================================================================

=> autorun.bat (Standard Windows Launcher & Compiler):

   * Standard Launch:
     Command: autorun.bat
     Action : Detects Go compiler in system PATH or default directories, checks 
              for pre-built binaries, builds if missing, and runs the application.

   * Force Recompile:
     Commands: autorun.bat -f
               autorun.bat --force
               autorun.bat -b
               autorun.bat --build
     Action  : Purges build artifacts, re-embeds the Windows UAC manifest using 
              `rsrc`, and generates a fresh `Cross-Suite_Wizard.exe`.

   * Universal Cross-Build from Windows:
     Commands: autorun.bat -a
               autorun.bat --all
               autorun.bat all
               autorun.bat cross
     Action  : Generates standalone binaries for Linux, macOS, and Windows into `.\dist\`.

=> autorun-hard.bat (Dedicated Windows Cache Purger):

   * Execution:
     Command: autorun-hard.bat (or double-click via File Explorer)
     Action : Invokes a full module cache invalidation (`go clean -modcache`), 
              clears system build cache, recompiles the Windows binary with embedded 
              manifests, and provides detailed error trapping upon exit.


==============================================================================
==>> 4. DEVELOPER ENGINE: Makefile CHEATSHEET
==============================================================================

For environments where `make` is preferred:

   make          - Compiles standard native local binary.
   make dev      - Downloads dependencies, updates modules, and builds fast host binary.
   make cross    - Compiles all 5 standalone OS/architecture binaries into `./dist/`.
   make release  - Generates distribution archives (.tar.gz and .zip) in `./dist/`.
   make install  - Installs binaries globally to `/usr/local/bin/` with permissions.
   make clean    - Removes all built binaries, distribution directories, and `.syso` files.


==============================================================================
==>> 5. AUTOMATED DEPENDENCY SELF-HEALING
==============================================================================

If the Go compiler is not detected on the machine:
* On Linux: `autorun.sh` checks the system package manager (`apt-get`, `dnf`, `yum`, 
  `pacman`, `zypper`, `apk`) and installs Go automatically. If package managers fail, 
  it downloads the official Go runtime tarball directly from `https://go.dev/dl/` 
  and provisions `/usr/local/go`.
* On macOS: `autorun.sh` attempts installation via `brew`, falling back to official 
  tarball deployment.
* On Windows: `autorun.bat` checks standard system directories and provides direct 
  guidance if no toolchain is found.